564 0 obj
<<
/Title (Repeating an idea is an act of generosity to your audience and to your band. It allows the listener to confirm an expectation, it enhances the power of the phrase, it allows the band to react and join the dialogue.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 433 0 R
/Prev 540 0 R
/Next 565 0 R
>>
endobj
565 0 obj
<<
/Title (So how can we practice that?)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 433 0 R
/Prev 564 0 R
/Next 541 0 R
>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<<
/Title (But there ONE rule you should follow:)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 494 0 R
/Prev 543 0 R
/Next 544 0 R
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Title (Repeat the chords, the gestures, the fragments of melody, the articulation. If you play a long phrase, try to repeat the last 2-3 notes.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 544 0 R
/Next 568 0 R
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Title (You will start to be surprised how suddenly out of that chaos some structure will emerge. REPETITION is ARCHITECTURE.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 544 0 R
/Prev 567 0 R
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Title (Miles Davis' Flamenco Sketches from "A Kind of Blue" \(1959\) is one of the most spectacular masterclasses on repetition, motives, and dialog with silence.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Next 570 0 R
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Title (The piece has no melody, just a sequence of 5 different modes over which each improviser takes a solo. Notice how Miles starts his solo by emphasizing the note G, and repeating 4 times a musical gesture that ends on G. On the last time, he gracefully ends on A to signify a conclusion of his first statement. Listen to the recording, and notice how simple it is.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Prev 569 0 R
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Title (Coltrane also embarks on a similar journey, repeating short 3-note motives three of four times on every new phrase.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 545 0 R
/Next 573 0 R
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Title (This may seem like a simple motive, but all motives are simple, THIS IS EXACTLY THE POINT. The art is not on how complex the motive is, but how you build upon it.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 574 0 R
/Next 546 0 R
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Title (Surely enough, Coltrane and Miles both embellish the motives, displace them in the rhythmic grid, change the rhythm, and add a few passing tones and ornaments, but in essence thay are repeating the same idea.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 571 0 R
/Next 575 0 R
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Title (CREATING  MOTIVES)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 576 0 R
/First 577 0 R
/Last 578 0 R
/Count -4
/Next 572 0 R
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Title (Are these motives incredibly brilliant? No. Just like Beethoven's 5th Symphony's motiff, they are quite basic. But the way they are being repeated is absolutely phenomenal.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 573 0 R
/Next 576 0 R
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Title (But are these repeated ideas "mistakes" or their "worst idea"? No. Miles and Coltrane are so intentional about their playing, that it's almost irrelavant to even talk about mistakes in their case.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 575 0 R
/Next 574 0 R
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Title (I often notice how students struggle to create simple motives. There is something frightening about creating a musical statement that is so short and simple. Inability to create good simple motives may lead to streams of never-ending phrases with no clear structure, like a confusing doodle.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 574 0 R
/Next 579 0 R
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Title (I         need    co-ffee)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 574 0 R
/Prev 580 0 R
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Title (Some jazz improvisation methods suggest thinking about direction: for example, choosing 3 notes in a specific direction \(up-up, up-down, down-up, down-down\).)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 574 0 R
/Prev 577 0 R
/Next 580 0 R
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Title (Personally, I prefer to think about a musical gesture inspired by words. So I'll choose a word or short sentence, say it out loud and try to emulate the sound musically. For example, let's think of the sentence: "I need coffee!"; I will repeat it out loud a few times, until its rhythm becomes clear:)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 574 0 R
/Prev 579 0 R
/First 581 0 R
/Last 581 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 578 0 R
>>
endobj
